course: arts
lesson: 40
title: Show day: the exhibition, the concert and the big arts quiz
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Show day brings the whole course together: paintings and clay on the walls and tables, an orchestra tuning up, a school play, a dance show, and a big quiz on everything from the Mona Lisa to Toy Story.
explain: The lessons of the course all meet here: art is made by people who practise, make mistakes and keep going; music, film, theatre and dance are teamwork; and almost everything around you was designed by someone.
explain: Show day also tests how you look at art: check the facts before you guess ("first", "biggest", "from where"), give credit, give kind and specific feedback, and remember that taste is argued.
explain: And if your clay owl cracks or you forget a line, you show it anyway and carry on, because the work and what you learned count, not only a perfect result.
explain: People say it their own way: "eh" in Canada means right?, "yaar" in India means mate, "bestie" means best friend, "stuffed" in Australia means broken, and "bare" in London means very.
fact: The Mona Lisa, painted by Leonardo da Vinci in the early 1500s, is quite small, about 77 by 53 centimetres, and hangs behind glass in the Louvre in Paris.
fact: The Mona Lisa became even more famous after it was stolen in 1911 and found two years later.
fact: Before a concert the orchestra tunes to one note, usually an A played by the oboe, so everyone plays in tune together.
fact: "Carol of the Bells" comes from "Shchedryk", a Ukrainian New Year song arranged by the composer Mykola Leontovych and sung in America by a Ukrainian choir in the early 1920s.
fact: The erhu is a Chinese instrument with two strings played with a bow, and it can sound almost like a singing voice; the Ukrainian bandura has dozens of strings and is plucked.
fact: Toy Story, by Pixar in 1995, was the first full-length film made entirely by computer animation; Shrek came later, in 2001.
fact: A film is many still pictures, called frames, shown quickly, traditionally 24 every second in the cinema.
fact: "Break a leg" is a theatre way of saying good luck.
fact: When an actor forgets a line, making something up to keep the scene going is called improvising, and audiences usually forgive it.
fact: Ballet began in the courts of Renaissance Italy and was developed in France, especially under King Louis XIV, which is why many ballet words are French.
fact: Cracks are common in pottery; the Japanese art of kintsugi even repairs broken pots with gold so the cracks become part of the beauty.
fact: Almost everything people make was designed by someone: chairs, signs, logos, books, phones, bags and clothes.
exercise: Zofia thinks the Mona Lisa covers a whole wall. How big is it really? | About 77 by 53 centimetres, quite small | About as big as a classroom wall | About as small as a postage stamp | About ten metres wide and five metres tall
exercise: What made the Mona Lisa even more famous in the 1900s? | It was stolen in 1911 and found two years later | It was painted again by a robot in 1911 | It was cut in half and sold to two museums | It was hidden in a cave in the mountains until the middle of last century
exercise: Lucas thinks the orchestra is "warming up badly" on one note. What are they doing? | Tuning together to one note, usually an A from the oboe | Practising the first note of the piece for good luck | Waiting for the conductor's phone to stop ringing | Showing which player has the loudest instrument
exercise: Which instrument usually gives the orchestra its tuning note? | The oboe | The triangle | The big bass drum | The piano tuner's phone
exercise: Danylo is asked which famous Christmas song comes from Ukraine. What is the answer? | "Carol of the Bells", from "Shchedryk" | "Jingle Bells", from a Kyiv choir | "Silent Night", from a Lviv church | "Last Christmas", from Ukrainian radio
exercise: Who arranged "Shchedryk"? | Mykola Leontovych | Taylor Swift | Leonardo da Vinci | Hayao Miyazaki
exercise: Lin's friend laughs that the erhu has only two strings. What is true? | Each instrument has its own sound and skill, whatever its strings | More strings always make a better instrument | Two strings means it is a toy for children | The other strings fell off just before the show and there was no time to fix them
exercise: Sam says Shrek was the first full-length computer-animated film. What is the right answer? | Toy Story, in 1995 | Shrek, in 2001 | Snow White, in 1937 | Wallace and Gromit, in 1989
exercise: Aisha says a film is "one long video". What does the strip of old film show? | Many still pictures shown quickly | One picture that slowly changes colour | A recording of sound only | A long painted scroll that is unrolled
exercise: How many frames per second are traditionally shown in a cinema? | 24 | 1 | 1,000 | 2
exercise: Niamh's da says "Break a leg!" and she panics. What does it mean? | Good luck in the theatre | Be careful on the stairs | Fall over on purpose for a laugh | Your acting is bad
exercise: Kian forgets his line and says something random, and the audience laughs. What is that called? | Improvising | Corpsing | Cheating out | Dubbing
exercise: Inès says ballet started in France; her friend says Italy. Who is right? | Both partly: it began in Italy and was developed in France | Only Inès, it is purely French | Only the friend, France had nothing to do with it | Neither of them, because ballet really started in Russia only last century
exercise: Why are many ballet words French? | Ballet was developed in France, especially under Louis XIV | French is the only language with words for dance moves | Ballet dancers must be French by law | The first ballet teacher could not speak Italian
exercise: Jack's clay owl cracked in the kiln. What is the wise move? | Show it and say what he learned | Hide it so nobody ever sees it | Smash it and say it was never made | Blame the kiln and the teacher
exercise: What is kintsugi? | Repairing pottery with gold | A Japanese two-string instrument | A dance at a summer festival | A way of folding paper cranes
exercise: Rohan says nothing in his school was designed. What is true? | Almost everything, from chairs to signs to his backpack, was designed | Only the posters on the walls were designed | Schools are built without any plans at all | Design only means fancy things sold in expensive shops, not school things
test: Your mate says, "The world's most famous painting must be massive, innit." What is the best answer? | Fame and size are different; the Mona Lisa is quite small | He is right, it fills a whole wall of the Louvre | It is so big it has its own building | Famous paintings are always the biggest ones, that is how they get noticed
test: What would happen if every orchestra player tuned to a different note? | The music would sound out of tune together | The music would sound richer and better | Nothing, because tuning does not matter | The conductor would play all the parts
test: In the big quiz, a kid shouts "Jingle Bells!" for the Ukrainian Christmas song. What is the correction? | "Carol of the Bells" comes from the Ukrainian "Shchedryk" | "Jingle Bells" was written in Kyiv in 1990 | No Christmas song comes from Ukraine | "Silent Night" is the Ukrainian one, first sung in a village church near Lviv
test: A Sydney kid says, "Only two strings? Mate, where's the rest?" What is a fair answer? | The erhu is made with two strings and played with a bow | The rest of the strings were lost on the plane | Two-string instruments are only for beginners | Real instruments must have at least six strings, like a guitar, to count
test: Sam from Atlanta says "Shrek, easy, no cap" ("no cap" means honestly). What should he have checked? | The dates: Toy Story came out in 1995, before Shrek | Which film had more songs in it | Which film made more money | Which film had a green main character
test: In a cartoon, a girl stops the film and sees only one frozen picture, then says films are magic. What is really true? | Films are many still pictures shown fast | Films are one moving picture that never stops | The film breaks when you pause it | Pictures move by themselves on the screen
test: Niamh says, "Why would ya want me to break my leg?" ("ya" means you). What is the answer? | It is a friendly theatre wish for good luck | Her da wants her to miss the show | It means she should dance wildly | It is a warning that the stage is broken and she might fall through it
test: Kian groans that his random line was "bare embarrassing" ("bare" means very). Which is true? | Improvising kept the scene going, and audiences forgive | One forgotten line ruins the whole play | He should have walked off the stage | Actors never forget lines, so he is the only one
test: A Florence kid says, "Nah, Italy, bestie" ("bestie" means best friend) about ballet. What is the fullest answer? | Italy and France are both part of ballet's history | Only Italy matters, France copied it | Only France matters, Italy never danced | Ballet was invented in America and only later brought over to Europe
test: Jack says his cracked owl is "stuffed" ("stuffed" means broken) and wants to hide it. What could he learn from kintsugi? | Cracks can be part of a piece's story and beauty | Cracked pots must always be thrown away | Only perfect work is allowed in exhibitions | Gold makes any pot more expensive than art
test: A Delhi kid says, "Nothing here is designed, yaar" ("yaar" means mate). What would a designer point at? | The chairs, signs and his own backpack | Only the paintings in the art room | Nothing, because schools are not designed | Only the teacher's expensive laptop
test: At the end of the course, which idea about art is truest? | People practise, make mistakes, work in teams and keep going | Art is only for people born with talent | Only famous artists make real art | Art is quick and easy for anyone who has the right app on their phone
exam: Identify the principal purpose of the single note sounded by an orchestra prior to a concert. | The tuning of all instruments to a common pitch | The rehearsal of the opening note of the work | The announcement of the conductor's arrival | The demonstration of which instrument in the orchestra is the loudest of all
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the dimensions of the Mona Lisa? | It is comparatively small, approximately 77 by 53 centimetres | It occupies an entire wall of the Louvre | It is smaller than a postage stamp | It measures approximately ten metres in width and five metres in height
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the origin of "Carol of the Bells". | It derives from "Shchedryk", a Ukrainian song arranged by Mykola Leontovych | It was composed in the United States in the present century | It derives from an Italian opera of the Renaissance | It was written for the film "Toy Story"
exam: Ascertain which of the following was the first full-length film produced entirely by computer animation. | "Toy Story", released in 1995 | "Shrek", released in 2001 | "King Kong", released in 1933 | "Spirited Away", released in 2001
